23:Quantifying individual differences in neuroimaging metrics is attracting interest in clinical studies with mental disorders. Schizophrenia is diagnosed exclusively based on symptoms, and the biological heterogeneity makes it difficult to accurately assess pharmacological treatment effects on the brain state. Using the Cambridge Centre for Ageing and Neuroscience data set, we built normative models of brain states and mapped the deviations of the brain characteristics of each patient, to test whether deviations were related to symptoms, and further investigated the pharmacological treatment effect on deviation distributions. Specifically, we found that the patients can be divided into 2 groups: the normalized group had a normalization trend and milder symptoms at baseline, and the other group showed a more severe deviation trend. The baseline severity of the depression as well as the overall symptoms could predict the deviation of the static characteristics for the dorsal and ventral attention networks after treatment. In contrast, the positive symptoms could predict the deviations of the dynamic fluctuations for the default mode and dorsal attention networks after treatment. This work evaluates the effect of pharmacological treatment on static and dynamic brain states using an individualized approach, which may assist in understanding the heterogeneity of the illness pathology as well as the treatment response.
29:Schizophrenia is one of the most complex and heterogeneous mental disorders. The antipsychotic treatment is the first-line treatment for patients with schizophrenia. Many studies have suggested that the treatment significantly affects brain structure and function, yet individual differences in treatment responses are ubiquitous and prominent (Lin et al. 2021; Mehta et al. 2021; Matrone et al. 2022). Up to 30% of schizophrenia patients do not respond or respond poorly to standard treatment with antipsychotics. However, existing techniques usually operate at the group level to get an average profile of those who respond or do not respond to medical treatment, and provide no path to individual-level inference. To move away from group average statistics, neuroscientists and psychiatrists have adopted the normal growth charts approach, trying to quantify individual differences in brain and symptoms in patients suffering from mental disorders. As a promising approach, normative modeling posits that psychopathology characteristics of patients with the same diagnosis are not homogeneous, it highlights the value of understanding individual variation relative to group means (Marquand et al. 2016; Rutherford et al. 2022a, 2022b). Normative modeling has been applied in computational psychiatry, detecting individuals who deviate from the norm, including autism spectrum disorder (Bethlehem et al. 2020; Shan et al. 2022), major depressive disorders (Lin et al. 2023), Alzheimer's disease (Pinaya et al. 2021; Verdi et al. 2023), ADHD (Wolfers et al. 2020), and schizophrenia (Wolfers et al. 2018; Lv et al. 2020; Chien et al. 2022). Based on the normative model, this work aimed to examine how the deviations of the dynamic and static brain states are modulated by antipsychotic treatment, and whether the baseline severity of symptoms could predict the longitudinal changes.
