 succeeded in 27ms:
111:dynamic (fFCS) FC, so 34 features represented each individual's dynamic FC pattern. Individualized deviation profiles of 68 features in SCZ patients (including before and after treatment, bSCZ:34, and aSCZ:34) were derived from sex-specific normative age models (separate models for female patients vs. male patients). For each patient, the features of the same age were positioned on the normative percentile curves from Cam-CAN to estimate individual deviation. We derived a Z -score with a scaling coefficient that quantified the deviation from the normative model for each feature as follows:
125:Table 1 shows the demographic and clinical characteristics of the study population. The data in the analysis consist of 37 adults with schizophrenia and 44 HCs. The data from the Cam-CAN data set were used to construct the sex-specific normative age model (as shown in Fig. 1C). And the comparison was made between the healthy comparison group and the patient's group. We also compared the distribution of the patients before and after 6-wk treatment.
145:Fig. 1. Methodological overview.A) Dynamic FC matrices for each individual between all pairs of regions of the Human Brainnetome Atlas were calculated using a sliding time window method (window length=60 s). B) Each FC matrix was decomposed into 17 networks based on the Yeo-17 network template and the features for each Yeo-17 network were yielded from time-varying dynamic FC matrices. C) The sex-specific normative age models for each dynamic FC feature were built based on Cam-CAN, and patients' features of the same age were positioned on the normative models to estimate individual deviation Z -scores. D) General linear models and Pearson's correlations of the deviation profiles on SCZ patients and clinical PANSS scores were conducted to explore the relationships between clinical symptom improvement and changes in dynamic FC patterns gaged with the deviation Z -scores.
